SNO	TOPIC	SIMPLE DEFINITION
1	Oracle DBA	A person responsible for installing, configuring, monitoring, securing, backing up, recovering, and maintaining Oracle databases.
2	Database	A structured collection of data stored and managed by a database management system.
3	Instance	The combination of Oracle memory structures (SGA) and background processes that access an Oracle database.
4	SGA	System Global Area	shared memory used by the Oracle instance for caching data, SQL, redo information, and other operations.
5	PGA	Program Global Area	private memory allocated to each server/background process.
6	Control File	A binary file containing important database metadata such as database name, datafile locations, and redo log information.
7	Datafile	Physical operating-system file that stores the actual database data.
8	Redo Log	Files that record changes made to the database and are essential for instance and media recovery.
9	Archive Log	A copy of filled redo logs created when the database is running in ARCHIVELOG mode.
10	Tablespace	A logical storage container that consists of one or more datafiles.
11	Schema	A collection of database objects owned by a database user, such as tables, indexes, views, and procedures.
12	User	An account used to connect to and access an Oracle database.
13	Listener	An Oracle networking process that receives incoming client connection requests and connects them to the database service.
14	ASM	Automatic Storage Management	Oracle's storage management solution for database files, providing disk management and striping/mirroring.
15	RAC	Real Application Clusters	multiple Oracle instances running on different nodes and accessing one database.
16	Data Guard	Oracle technology used to maintain one or more standby databases for high availability and disaster recovery.
17	Primary Database	The main production database that generates redo and sends it to standby databases.
18	Standby Database	A synchronized copy of the primary database used mainly for disaster recovery and high availability.
19	RMAN	Recovery Manager	Oracle's tool for database backup, restore, and recovery.
20	Backup	A copy of database files or database information used to restore the database after failure or data loss.
21	Recovery	The process of applying redo/archived logs and restoring database files to recover the database to a consistent state.
22	Cloning	Creating a copy of an existing database for development, testing, reporting, or other purposes.
23	Patching	Applying Oracle fixes and security updates to the Oracle software or database.
24	Upgrade	Moving an Oracle database from an older version to a newer supported version.
25	Migration	Moving a database from one server, platform, storage system, or environment to another.
26	Switchover	A planned role reversal between a primary and standby database with minimal or no data loss.
27	Failover	Changing the standby database into the primary database when the original primary database becomes unavailable.
28	AWR	Automatic Workload Repository	stores database performance statistics used for performance analysis.
29	ASH	Active Session History	captures information about active database sessions and their wait events.
30	ADDM	Automatic Database Diagnostic Monitor	analyzes AWR data and provides recommendations for performance problems.
31	SQL Tuning	The process of improving SQL execution performance by analyzing execution plans, indexes, statistics, and SQL structure.
32	Index	A database object that helps Oracle find rows more efficiently without scanning the entire table.
33	Wait Event	An event showing what a database session is waiting for, such as I/O, locks, or CPU-related resources.
34	Deadlock	A situation where two or more sessions wait for resources locked by each other, preventing progress.
35	UNDO	Storage used to maintain previous versions of changed data, supporting rollback, read consistency, and recovery.
36	TEMP Tablespace	A temporary storage area used for operations such as sorting, joins, and temporary result processing.
37	SPFILE	Server Parameter File; a binary file containing persistent Oracle initialization parameters.
38	PFILE	Text-based initialization parameter file used to start an Oracle instance.
39	Database Link	A database object that allows one Oracle database to access objects in another database.
40	OEM	Oracle Enterprise Manager	a graphical management tool used to monitor and administer Oracle environments.
